The distance table here stores the cheapest price for every pair of city and flight count. That makes the stop limit part of the state instead of keeping only one price per city.

For each state, the code tries every outgoing flight and records the result at the next flight count. The answer is the smallest value for the destination across all allowed counts.

Important edge cases are handled: a direct flight works when `K` is zero, and an unreachable destination returns `-1`. The input slice is only read.

```go
func CheapestFlight(n int, flights [][]int, src int, dst int, K int) int {
    const infinity = int(1 << 60)
    maxFlights := K + 1

    cost := make([][]int, n)
    for city := 0; city < n; city++ {
        cost[city] = make([]int, maxFlights+1)
        for used := 0; used <= maxFlights; used++ {
            cost[city][used] = infinity
        }
    }
    cost[src][0] = 0

    for used := 0; used < maxFlights; used++ {
        for _, flight := range flights {
            from := flight[0]
            to := flight[1]
            price := flight[2]

            if cost[from][used] == infinity {
                continue
            }

            nextCost := cost[from][used] + price
            if nextCost < cost[to][used+1] {
                cost[to][used+1] = nextCost
            }
        }
    }

    answer := infinity
    for used := 0; used <= maxFlights; used++ {
        if cost[dst][used] < answer {
            answer = cost[dst][used]
        }
    }

    if answer == infinity {
        return -1
    }
    return answer
}
```

The `int` constant used for infinity is large enough for normal price totals and avoids adding to a small sentinel value.

Time complexity: `O((K + 1) * E)`.
Space complexity: `O(n * (K + 1))`.

This is a small depth-first search. It tries every route that still has flights available and keeps the cheapest answer found so far. A route is stopped early when its current cost is already no better than the answer.

It changes no input data. The main trade-off is that DFS can visit many paths, so this is easier to follow than it is to scale on a dense graph.

Dry run: with two direct choices costing 500 and 200, DFS records 500 first, then replaces it with 200. A later branch costing more than 200 is ignored before it is expanded.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

class Solution {
    private int answer;
    private List<List<int[]>> graph;

    public int CheapestFlight(int n, List<List<int>> flights, int src, int dst, int K) {
        graph = new List<List<int[]>>();
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            graph.Add(new List<int[]>());
        }

        foreach (List<int> flight in flights) {
            graph[flight[0]].Add(new int[] {flight[1], flight[2]});
        }

        answer = int.MaxValue;
        Search(src, dst, K + 1, 0);

        return answer == int.MaxValue ? -1 : answer;
    }

    private void Search(int city, int destination, int flightsLeft, int cost) {
        if (city == destination) {
            answer = Math.Min(answer, cost);
            return;
        }

        if (flightsLeft == 0) {
            return;
        }

        foreach (int[] edge in graph[city]) {
            int nextCity = edge[0];
            int nextCost = cost + edge[1];

            if (nextCost < answer) {
                Search(nextCity, destination, flightsLeft - 1, nextCost);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

The destination can be reached directly when `K` is zero because the search starts with `K + 1` available flights.

Time complexity: exponential in the worst case, about `O(E^(K + 1))` for a dense branching graph.
Space complexity: `O(n + E + K)` including the graph and recursion stack.

A min-heap is useful when cheap routes should be checked first. Each heap item contains the total cost, current city, and number of flights already used. The stop count cannot be left out, since two visits to the same city may have different remaining limits.

The code returns as soon as the destination is removed from the heap. Since heap entries are ordered by price, that first valid destination entry is the cheapest one.

```cpp
#include <queue>
#include <tuple>
#include <vector>
#include <climits>
#include <functional>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    int CheapestFlight(int n, vector<vector<int>> &flights,
                       int src, int dst, int K) {
        vector<vector<pair<int, int>>> graph(n);
        for (const vector<int>& flight : flights) {
            graph[flight[0]].push_back({flight[1], flight[2]});
        }

        using State = tuple<int, int, int>;
        priority_queue<State, vector<State>, greater<State>> heap;
        heap.push({0, src, 0});

        vector<int> fewestFlights(n, INT_MAX);
        fewestFlights[src] = 0;

        while (!heap.empty()) {
            auto [cost, city, usedFlights] = heap.top();
            heap.pop();

            if (city == dst) {
                return cost;
            }

            if (usedFlights == K + 1) {
                continue;
            }

            if (usedFlights > fewestFlights[city]) {
                continue;
            }
            fewestFlights[city] = usedFlights;

            for (auto [nextCity, ticketPrice] : graph[city]) {
                heap.push({cost + ticketPrice, nextCity, usedFlights + 1});
            }
        }

        return -1;
    }
};
```

The `K + 1` check is because `K` counts intermediate stops, while the route can contain one more flight than that. All prices are positive, so the heap ordering is safe.

Time complexity: up to `O(EK log(EK))` in the bounded state search.
Space complexity: `O(EK + n)` in the worst case for heap states and graph storage.

This version treats each BFS level as one more flight. The queue stores the city and the price used to reach it. A separate array for the next level keeps a cheaper path from accidentally using two flights during one level.

The first level checks direct flights, and the next level checks routes with one intermediate city. If no route reaches the destination, the method returns `-1`.

```java
import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Queue;

class Solution {
    public int CheapestFlight(int n, int[][] flights, int src, int dst, int K) {
        List<List<int[]>> graph = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            graph.add(new ArrayList<>());
        }

        for (int[] flight : flights) {
            graph.get(flight[0]).add(new int[] {flight[1], flight[2]});
        }

        int[] best = new int[n];
        Arrays.fill(best, Integer.MAX_VALUE);
        best[src] = 0;

        Queue<int[]> queue = new ArrayDeque<>();
        queue.offer(new int[] {src, 0});

        int flightsAllowed = K + 1;
        while (!queue.isEmpty() && flightsAllowed-- > 0) {
            int levelSize = queue.size();
            int[] nextBest = best.clone();

            while (levelSize-- > 0) {
                int[] state = queue.poll();
                int city = state[0];
                int currentCost = state[1];

                for (int[] edge : graph.get(city)) {
                    int nextCity = edge[0];
                    int newCost = currentCost + edge[1];

                    if (newCost < nextBest[nextCity]) {
                        nextBest[nextCity] = newCost;
                        queue.offer(new int[] {nextCity, newCost});
                    }
                }
            }

            best = nextBest;
        }

        return best[dst] == Integer.MAX_VALUE ? -1 : best[dst];
    }
}
```

For example, when `K = 0`, `flightsAllowed` is 1, so only direct flights are examined. Empty flight lists also work because the destination stays at infinity.

Time complexity: `O((K + 1) * E)`.
Space complexity: `O(n + E)` for the graph, arrays, and queue.

